DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The uunnvviiss(), ssttrruunnvviiss() and ssttrruunnvviissxx() functions are used to decode a
     visual representation of characters, as produced by the vis(3) function,
     back into the original form.

     The uunnvviiss() function is called with successive characters in _c until a
     valid sequence is recognized, at which time the decoded character is
     available at the character pointed to by _c_p.

     The ssttrruunnvviiss() function decodes the characters pointed to by _s_r_c into the
     buffer pointed to by _d_s_t.  The ssttrruunnvviiss() function simply copies _s_r_c to
     _d_s_t, decoding any escape sequences along the way, and returns the number
     of characters placed into _d_s_t, or -1 if an invalid escape sequence was
     detected.  The size of _d_s_t should be equal to the size of _s_r_c (that is,
     no expansion takes place during decoding).

     The ssttrruunnvviissxx() and ssttrrnnuunnvviissxx() functions do the same as the ssttrruunnvviiss()
     and ssttrrnnuunnvviiss() functions, but take a flag that specifies the style the
     string _s_r_c is encoded with.  The meaning of the flag is the same as ex‐
     plained below for uunnvviiss().

     The uunnvviiss() function implements a state machine that can be used to de‐
     code an arbitrary stream of bytes.  All state associated with the bytes
     being decoded is stored outside the uunnvviiss() function (that is, a pointer
     to the state is passed in), so calls decoding different streams can be
     freely intermixed.  To start decoding a stream of bytes, first initialize
     an integer to zero.  Call uunnvviiss() with each successive byte, along with a
     pointer to this integer, and a pointer to a destination character.  The
     uunnvviiss() function has several return codes that must be handled properly.
     They are:

     0 (zero)         Another character is necessary; nothing has been recog‐
                      nized yet.

     UNVIS_VALID      A valid character has been recognized and is available
                      at the location pointed to by _c_p.

     UNVIS_VALIDPUSH  A valid character has been recognized and is available
                      at the location pointed to by _c_p; however, the character
                      currently passed in should be passed in again.

     UNVIS_NOCHAR     A valid sequence was detected, but no character was pro‐
                      duced.  This return code is necessary to indicate a log‐
                      ical break between characters.

     UNVIS_SYNBAD     An invalid escape sequence was detected, or the decoder
                      is in an unknown state.  The decoder is placed into the
                      starting state.

     When all bytes in the stream have been processed, call uunnvviiss() one more
     time with flag set to UNVIS_END to extract any remaining character (the
     character passed in is ignored).

     The _f_l_a_g argument is also used to specify the encoding style of the
     source.  If set to VIS_NOESCAPE uunnvviiss() will not decode backslash es‐
     capes.  If set to VIS_HTTPSTYLE or VIS_HTTP1808, uunnvviiss() will decode URI
     strings as specified in RFC 1808.  If set to VIS_HTTP1866, uunnvviiss() will
     decode entity references and numeric character references as specified in
     RFC 1866.  If set to VIS_MIMESTYLE, uunnvviiss() will decode MIME Quoted-
     Printable strings as specified in RFC 2045.  If set to VIS_NOESCAPE,
     uunnvviiss() will not decode ‘\’ quoted characters.

     The following code fragment illustrates a proper use of uunnvviiss().

           int state = 0;
           char out;

           while ((ch = getchar()) != EOF) {
           again:
                   switch(unvis(&out, ch, &state, 0)) {
                   case 0:
                   case UNVIS_NOCHAR:
                           break;
                   case UNVIS_VALID:
                           (void)putchar(out);
                           break;
                   case UNVIS_VALIDPUSH:
                           (void)putchar(out);
                           goto again;
                   case UNVIS_SYNBAD:
                           errx(EXIT_FAILURE, "Bad character sequence!");
                   }
           }
           if (unvis(&out, '\0', &state, UNVIS_END) == UNVIS_VALID)
                   (void)putchar(out);

EERRRROORRSS
     The functions ssttrruunnvviiss(), ssttrrnnuunnvviiss(), ssttrruunnvviissxx(), and ssttrrnnuunnvviissxx() will
     return -1 on error and set _e_r_r_n_o to:

     [EINVAL]           An invalid escape sequence was detected, or the de‐
                        coder is in an unknown state.

     In addition the functions ssttrrnnuunnvviiss() and ssttrrnnuunnvviissxx() will can also set
     _e_r_r_n_o on error to:

     [ENOSPC]           Not enough space to perform the conversion.

